course: biology
lesson: 180
title: The Galápagos: giant tortoises, sea iguanas, Darwin's finches and tame sea lions
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Galápagos are volcanic islands in the Pacific Ocean on the equator, far from the coast of South America, and the animals that reached them long ago slowly changed to fit each island.
explain: So you find a lizard that swims in the sea and eats seaweed, a cormorant that cannot fly, penguins on the equator and tortoises whose shells are shaped to stretch up for cactus.
explain: Because people hardly ever hunted them, the animals are not afraid: a sea lion may nap on a park bench while you walk past, like a neighbour's cat that does not bother to move.
explain: In 1835 Charles Darwin visited on the ship Beagle and noticed that birds and tortoises were a little different on each island, and those clues helped him explain how living things change over many generations.
fact: Galápagos giant tortoises are among the largest tortoises and can live over a hundred years; on dry islands their saddle-shaped shells let them stretch up to tall cactus.
fact: Lonesome George, the last tortoise of his kind from Pinta Island, died in 2012.
fact: The marine iguana is the only lizard that swims and feeds in the sea; it dives for seaweed, sneezes out extra salt, and can shrink its body, even its bones, in hard years.
fact: Darwin's finches have beaks shaped for different foods, thick for seeds and thin for insects, and birds with the best beaks for their island's food survived and had more young.
fact: Peter and Rosemary Grant watched the finches' beaks change within just a few years after a drought.
fact: Male blue-footed boobies show off their blue feet in a dance, and brighter blue feet show a healthy, well-fed bird that females prefer.
fact: The flightless cormorant is the only cormorant that cannot fly; with no land predators and food in the sea, its wings shrank over many generations.
fact: The Galápagos penguin is the only penguin that lives north of the equator in the wild, because cold currents bring cool, fish-rich water to the islands.
fact: Galápagos sea lions, which have four flippers, nap on benches and boats because the animals were never hunted much, and visitors must stay two metres away.
fact: Darwin visited the Galápagos on the Beagle in 1835, and the differences between islands helped him explain evolution in his book of 1859.
fact: Male frigatebirds puff up a big red throat pouch to attract females, and frigatebirds can stay in the air for weeks without landing.
fact: Goats brought by people ate the plants tortoises needed; when people removed goats from many islands, plants and tortoises began to come back.
fact: Sally Lightfoot crabs are bright red crabs that leap from rock to rock, eat algae and pick ticks off marine iguanas.
exercise: Why do some Galápagos tortoises have saddle-shaped shells? | The shape lets them stretch their necks up to tall cactus | It helps them swim faster from one island to the next in storms | It keeps rain off their eggs | It lets them roll down hills
exercise: How long can Galápagos giant tortoises live? | Over a hundred years | About ten years | About one year | Exactly fifty years and not one day more
exercise: Who was Lonesome George? | The last tortoise of his kind from Pinta Island | The captain of the Beagle who brought Darwin there | A famous sea lion | The first finch Darwin saw
exercise: What makes the marine iguana unique? | It is the only lizard that swims and feeds in the sea | It is the only lizard that can fly from island to island | It lives only in snow | It eats only fish
exercise: How does a marine iguana get rid of extra salt? | It sneezes it out from special glands | It sweats it out | It keeps it stored in its long tail | It never swallows any salt at all, even by mistake
exercise: Why do Darwin's finches have different beaks? | Birds whose beaks best fit the food on their island survived and had more young | Each bird chooses a new beak every year | People clipped their beaks | Beaks change colour, not shape
exercise: What did Peter and Rosemary Grant see after a drought? | Finch beaks changing within a few years | Finches slowly turning into iguanas | All the finches flying away for good, never to return | Tortoises growing wings
exercise: Why does a blue-footed booby show off its feet? | Bright blue feet show it is healthy and well fed | To scare away hungry sharks when it dives for fish | To keep its feet cool | Because they are cold
exercise: Why can the flightless cormorant not fly? | With no land predators and food in the sea, its wings shrank over generations | It broke its wings fighting | It is too young | Its feathers are made of stone
exercise: How can a penguin live on the equator in the Galápagos? | Cold currents bring cool, fish-rich water to the islands | It lives inside cool caves deep under the islands, like a bat | It eats only ice | The equator is always freezing
exercise: Why are Galápagos animals not afraid of people? | They were never hunted much | They are all blind | They are trained by keepers | They are made of plastic
exercise: How far should visitors stay from Galápagos animals? | Two metres | Two centimetres | Two kilometres | They may hug them
exercise: When did Darwin visit the Galápagos? | In 1835 on the ship Beagle | In 1935 in a plane | In 1492 with Columbus on the Santa María | He never went there
exercise: What does a male frigatebird puff up to attract females? | A big red throat pouch | Its blue feet | Its tail feathers into a fan | A balloon of seaweed
exercise: Why did goats harm Galápagos tortoises? | They ate the plants tortoises needed faster than plants could grow | They attacked the tortoises with their horns and knocked them all over | They stole tortoise eggs | They drank all the sea water
exercise: How do Sally Lightfoot crabs help marine iguanas? | They pick ticks off them | They carry them on their backs | They sing to them | They bring them seaweed
test: «3 blue-footed boobies dancing on a rock, how many blue feet» | 6 | 3 | 9 | 12
test: «2 giant tortoises and a sea lion, how many legs and flippers altogether» | 12 | 10 | 8 | 14
test: In the film Madagascar, penguins live in a city zoo. Where do penguins live wild north of the equator? | Only on the Galápagos | In New York | In the Sahara | At the North Pole, on the ice
test: «penguins only live in the ice, that's literally the rule» What is right? | Galápagos penguins live on the equator, cooled by cold currents | Right, they all live on ice | Penguins live only in zoos | Penguins live only in jungles
test: What would happen to the tortoises if goats came back to an island? | The goats would eat their plants and the tortoises would go hungry | The tortoises would ride the goats | Nothing at all | The tortoises would eat the goats and grow much bigger and stronger
test: «Darwin saw a finch turn into another bird overnight» What is right? | Changes happen over many generations, though beaks can shift within a few years | Right, it took one night | Finches never change at all | Darwin never saw any finches
test: «sea lion on the bench, go on, give him a cuddle» What should you do? | Stay two metres away and leave him alone | Hug him gently | Sit on him | Throw him a sandwich and take a quick selfie
test: A cartoon lizard swims under the sea and eats fish. What does the real marine iguana eat? | Seaweed | Fish | Sand | Crabs only
test: «those birds have tiny wings, they must be babies» What is more likely on the Galápagos? | They are flightless cormorants, whose wings shrank over generations | Babies that will grow huge wings | Penguins in disguise | Bats
test: In a hard year with little food, how can a marine iguana survive? | It can shrink its body, even its bones | It flies off to another island where there is food | It grows extra legs | It eats rocks
test: «a red crab just jumped onto an iguana's back, is it attacking» | It is probably picking off ticks | It wants to eat the iguana | It is trying to fly with the iguana | It is lost
exam: Identify the characteristic which distinguishes the marine iguana from all other lizards. | It alone swims in the sea and feeds there upon seaweed | It alone is capable of flight | It alone inhabits polar ice | It alone consumes fish exclusively
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the variation in the beaks of Darwin's finches? | Beaks are adapted to different foods, through the survival of the best-suited birds over generations | Each bird alters its beak at will | The beaks were modified by sailors | All finches possess identical beaks
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Galápagos penguin is able to inhabit equatorial waters. | Cold ocean currents supply cool water abundant in fish | The islands are permanently covered in thick ice and snow | It subsists without food | It resides solely in shaded caves
exam: State the year in which Charles Darwin visited the Galápagos Islands and the vessel upon which he travelled. | 1835, aboard the Beagle | 1859, aboard the Endeavour | 1492, aboard the Santa María | 1901, aboard the Discovery
